Paragraph for your README
This web application is a simple equation visualizer built using HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and the Plotly.js visualization library. The application allows users to input a mathematical expression in terms of x and generate a graph of the function. When the user enters an equation and clicks the plot button, JavaScript reads the input value from the text field and removes any optional y = part using a regular expression (equation.replace(/y\s*=\s*/, "")). The program then generates a range of x-values from −10 to 10 using a loop, which provides a reasonable viewing window that includes both negative and positive parts of the graph. For each x value, the program calculates the corresponding y value by evaluating the expression using eval(). These (x, y) pairs are stored in arrays and passed to Plotly, which renders the graph dynamically inside a <div> element. The application also supports two visualization modes: line mode, which connects points into a smooth curve, and scatter mode, which displays individual data points using markers. Error handling is implemented using a try...catch block to detect invalid mathematical expressions and prevent the program from crashing. A clear function using Plotly.purge() removes the existing graph from the container and resets the plot area. While eval() is used here for simplicity, it is generally avoided in production systems because it executes arbitrary JavaScript code and can introduce security vulnerabilities. Professional graphing applications typically use mathematical expression parsers such as math.js to safely interpret user-entered equations.
